      <section id="section-2">
        <h2>Volatile vs Non-Volatile Silicone Types for Hair</h2>
        <p>Choosing the right silicone type is the central formulation decision in an aerosol dry conditioner, because volatility and film character trade off directly against each other. Volatile cyclic silicones, most commonly <a href="https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/compound/Cyclopentasiloxane" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">cyclopentasiloxane (D5)</a>, evaporate from the hair within minutes and leave only a very light conditioning effect, which makes them useful as a spreading aid rather than a long-wear film former. Non-volatile linear silicones such as dimethicone stay on the hair indefinitely, building a durable, higher-gloss film that persists through brushing and styling. <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silicone" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Silicones</a> in general are built on a silicon-oxygen backbone that gives them the flexibility and low surface energy behind this lubricating behaviour.</p>
        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table class="data-table">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th>Silicone Type</th>
                <th>INCI Name</th>
                <th>Volatility</th>
                <th>Typical Role</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
