      <section id="section-5">
        <h2>Performance Testing: Combing Force &amp; Gloss</h2>
        <p>Formulators verify detangling performance with instrumented combing force testing rather than relying on subjective feel alone. A comb is drawn through a standardised hair tress at a controlled speed while a force gauge records the peak and average resistance, and the treated tress is compared against an untreated control cut from the same source hair. A validated formulation produces a measurable, repeatable reduction in combing force on both wet and dry hair, not just an immediate-use sensory improvement that fades after a few brush strokes.</p>
        <p>Gloss is measured separately with a goniophotometer, which quantifies how much light reflects off the hair surface at a fixed angle — a smoother, more uniform silicone film reflects light more specularly and scores higher. Silicone deposition itself can be quantified gravimetrically or by extracting and analysing the film left on a treated tress, giving formulators a direct read on how much of the applied silicone actually stayed on the hair versus how much was lost to overspray or evaporation of the carrier.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="section-6">
        <h2>Regulatory, Stability &amp; VOC Considerations</h2>
        <p>An aerosol dry conditioner is regulated as a cosmetic rather than a drug in most markets, which places the compliance burden on accurate ingredient labelling and substantiated claims rather than premarket drug approval. In the United States, labelling and safety expectations for leave-on hair products are set out by the <a href="https://www.fda.gov/cosmetics" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">FDA</a>, while Volatile Organic Compound content from the carrier and propellant is subject to the same regional consumer-product VOC limits that apply across the wider <a href="https://www.globalaerosols.com/cosmetic-aerosol-manufacturing/" target="_top">cosmetics and personal care aerosol manufacturing</a> category.</p>
        <p>Cyclic volatile silicones carry their own regulatory attention: octamethylcyclotetrasiloxane (D4) and decamethylcyclopentasiloxane (D5) are identified as persistent, bioaccumulative substances under EU REACH, which restricts their use above defined concentration thresholds in cosmetic products sold in the European market. Formulators selling into the EU substitute D5 with alternative volatile carriers and recheck the current ECHA position before finalising a recipe, the same compliance step already required for other silicone-based personal care aerosols such as <a href="https://www.globalaerosols.com/personal-care-aerosol-deoderant-and-body-sprays/" target="_top">personal care deodorant and body sprays</a>. Stability testing stores the finished product at elevated temperature to confirm the silicone stays dissolved or stably dispersed and that the cationic deposition aid does not interact unfavourably with other charged ingredients in the formula over shelf life.</p>
      </section>

